## Permitted Purpose

Any purpose is a permitted purpose, except the two below. The purposes listed
in [Always Permitted](#always-permitted) are permitted even when the second
applies.

1. providing to others any product that competes with the software (see
   [Noncompete](#noncompete)); and

2. any use for the benefit of a Large Company (see
   [Revenue Limit](#revenue-limit)), unless that company holds a commercial
   license for the software from the licensor.

## Noncompete

Providing to others any product that competes with the software is never a
permitted purpose under these terms, whatever the size of your company.

## Competition

If you use this software to market a product as a substitute for the
functionality or value of the software, it competes with the software. A
product also competes with the software if it substitutes for any other product
or service the licensor offers using the software that exists on the date the
licensor makes this version of the software available. A product may compete
regardless how it is designed or deployed. For example, a product may compete
even if it provides its functionality via any kind of interface (including
services, libraries or plug-ins), even if it is ported to a different platform
or programming language, and even if it is provided free of charge.

## Revenue Limit

Use of the software for the benefit of your company is a permitted purpose only
if your company is not a Large Company.

Your company is a **Large Company** if its Annual Revenue was 10,000,000 USD or
more, or the equivalent in other currencies, at any time in the prior twelve
months.

**Annual Revenue** is the greater of:

1. your company's total gross revenue, from all sources, in its most recently
   completed fiscal year; and

2. your company's annual recurring revenue at that time, being its monthly
   recurring revenue from subscriptions, contracts and other recurring
   arrangements, multiplied by twelve.

Annual Revenue is counted across your company as a whole, including every
organization in it under the definition of **your company** below.

A Large Company may get a commercial license by contacting the licensor at
https://executionassociates.com. Use by a Large Company without one is not
covered by your licenses, and the licensor may treat it as a violation under
[Violations](#violations).

If your company becomes a Large Company, your use of the software for its
benefit may continue for 32 days after it first does so while you seek a
commercial license.

## Always Permitted

Whatever the size of your company, these are always permitted purposes, so long
as they do not compete with the software:

1. use by an individual for personal, non-commercial purposes;

2. non-commercial education;

3. non-commercial research;

4. evaluating the software, for up to 32 consecutive days; and

5. professional services you provide to a licensee who is using the software
   for a permitted purpose.

